MOS Persistent Cognitive Substrate (PCS)
Specification v0.1
Part III — Physical Storage Model (PSM)
Abstract

The Physical Storage Model defines how Cognitive Objects are organized, persisted, indexed, replicated, and maintained by a storage implementation.

Unlike the Logical Storage Model, which defines semantics, the Physical Storage Model defines operational behavior while remaining independent of any specific storage technology.

The PSM establishes a common implementation contract that allows multiple storage engines to implement the Persistent Cognitive Substrate consistently.

Chapter 1
Purpose

The Physical Storage Model bridges logical cognition and concrete storage technologies.

It specifies:

physical organization,
storage collections,
indexing,
partitioning,
transactions,
consistency,
durability,
distribution,
optimization.

Implementations SHALL preserve logical semantics while optimizing physical storage.

Chapter 2
Physical Architecture

The PSM is composed of the following layers:

Persistent Cognitive Substrate
            │
            ▼
   Logical Storage Model
            │
            ▼
  Physical Storage Model
            │
 ┌──────────┼──────────┐
 ▼          ▼          ▼
Collections Indexes  Partitions
            │
            ▼
 Storage Engine Backend

The Physical Storage Model acts as an implementation contract rather than a database schema.

Chapter 3
Storage Collections

A Storage Collection groups Storage Objects with similar physical characteristics.

Unlike a Storage Domain, which groups objects semantically, a Storage Collection groups them operationally.

A Storage Collection defines:

access pattern,
indexing strategy,
partitioning policy,
compression policy,
retention policy,
replication policy,
durability guarantees.

Collections are logical constructs and SHALL NOT be assumed to map one-to-one to database tables.

Chapter 4
Physical Object Layout

Every persisted object SHALL consist of standardized physical sections:

Object Header
    │
    ├── PID
    ├── Type
    ├── Version
    ├── Domain
    ├── Class
    ├── Policy
    │
Object Metadata
    │
    ├── Provenance
    ├── Temporal Fields
    ├── Security Context
    │
Object Payload
    │
Object Relationships

This layout is conceptual and independent of serialization format.

Chapter 5
Persistence Policies

Storage behavior is governed by Persistence Policies.

Examples include:

Policy	Description
Hot	Low-latency operational data
Warm	Frequently accessed persistent knowledge
Cold	Infrequently accessed online storage
Frozen	Immutable archive
Replicated	Multi-node durability
Encrypted	Additional confidentiality
Pinned	Exempt from lifecycle optimization

Policies MAY change throughout an object's lifecycle without altering its identity.

Chapter 6
Indexing Model

Indexes SHALL be treated as derived structures.

Indexes SHALL NOT contain authoritative knowledge.

They exist solely to accelerate retrieval.

Examples:

Identity indexes
Relationship indexes
Temporal indexes
Semantic indexes
Spatial indexes
Full-text indexes
Vector indexes

Indexes SHALL be rebuildable from authoritative Storage Objects.

Chapter 7
Partitioning

Storage Collections MAY be partitioned according to:

time,
tenant,
domain,
environment,
geography,
lifecycle state,
workload.

Partitioning SHALL preserve logical transparency.

Applications SHALL not require knowledge of partition boundaries.

Chapter 8
Transactions

The PSM requires atomicity for logically related updates.

Examples include:

creating an Entity and its initial Facts,
superseding a Fact while preserving provenance,
completing a Plan and archiving associated Tasks.

Implementations MAY use different transactional mechanisms, provided logical consistency is preserved.

Chapter 9
Consistency

The PSM distinguishes:

Transactional Consistency
Referential Consistency
Temporal Consistency
Cognitive Consistency

Not all operations require the same consistency guarantees.

The required consistency level SHALL be explicit.

Chapter 10
Replication and Distribution

Storage implementations MAY replicate data across nodes.

Replication SHALL preserve:

identity,
provenance,
version history,
temporal semantics.

Conflict resolution SHALL never discard historical information.

Chapter 11
Durability

Persistent objects SHALL survive:

process restart,
node restart,
storage migration,
software upgrade.

Durability SHALL be configurable according to Persistence Policy.

Chapter 12
Optimization

Optimizations SHALL never alter semantic meaning.

Permitted optimizations include:

compression,
deduplication,
index rebuilding,
archival migration,
storage tiering.

All optimizations SHALL preserve explainability and provenance.

Chapter 13
Recovery

The PSM SHALL support recovery of:

individual objects,
collections,
domains,
complete cognitive state.

Recovery SHALL preserve historical versions and relationships.

Chapter 14
Explainability

Physical implementation details SHALL never prevent reconstruction of logical cognition.

Storage optimizations SHALL remain transparent to explainability.

Chapter 15
Compliance

A Physical Storage Model implementation complies with PCS Part III if it provides:

Storage Collections.
Standardized object layout.
Persistence Policies.
Derived indexes.
Transparent partitioning.
Explicit consistency guarantees.
Durable persistence.
Recoverability.
Explainability preservation.
