A refinement that emerged from Part III

While defining Storage Collections and Persistence Policies, another pattern became clear:

Storage Capabilities

Instead of assuming every backend supports every feature, MOS should define a capability negotiation model.

A backend advertises the capabilities it implements, for example:

Capability	Purpose
Versioning	Immutable object history
Vector Search	Embedding similarity
Graph Traversal	Native relationship queries
Temporal Queries	Time-travel and valid-time lookups
Full-Text Search	Lexical retrieval
ACID Transactions	Strong transactional guarantees
Distributed Replication	Multi-node synchronization
Object Compression	Space optimization
Encryption at Rest	Confidential persistence

This means the MOS API can target the abstract capability rather than a specific database. A PostgreSQL implementation might provide all capabilities through extensions (pgvector, Apache AGE, TimescaleDB, etc.), while another backend could provide only a subset.

I believe this will be a defining architectural decision because it decouples the what from the how. The API asks for "semantic vector search" or "time-travel query"; the backend advertises whether it supports that capability and how it fulfills it. This makes MOS portable without weakening the specification.
